(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-hf-diuretics-acei-beta-blockers-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Heart Failure: Diuretics, ACE Inhibitors & Beta Blockers Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which electrolyte change can angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors cause?","choices":["Low serum potassium","Elevated serum potassium","Low serum calcium","High serum chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These drugs raise serum potassium, so levels are watched, especially with other potassium-raising drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which effect on the kidney can angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors cause in heart failure?","choices":["Impaired renal function","Kidney stones","Excessive urine volume","Improved filtration in every patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Impairment of renal function is a recognized problem with these drugs. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient has symptoms of heart failure with a preserved ejection fraction. Which type is this?","choices":["Diastolic dysfunction","Systolic dysfunction","Right-sided valve disease","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure symptoms with preserved ejection fraction reflect impaired relaxation and filling, or diastolic dysfunction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measure may improve functional status in heart failure?","choices":["Physical activity","Strict bed rest","Avoiding all exertion","Fluid loading"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Physical activity may improve functional status in patients with heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which benefit do beta blockers provide in heart failure?","choices":["Immediate relief of congestion","Fewer hospitalizations","An immediate rise in contractility","Prevention of sodium retention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers decrease hospitalizations and the need for transplant, and lower mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does ischemia contribute to diastolic dysfunction?","choices":["Calcium entry into the cell is blocked","Sodium is retained by the kidney","Calcium removal from the cytosol is impaired","Contraction becomes too forceful"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ischemia impairs return of calcium from the cytosol to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, so the muscle relaxes poorly. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which home measurement best detects worsening fluid overload in heart failure?","choices":["Daily oral temperature","Morning blood glucose","Urine color","Daily body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A rising weight over several days reflects fluid accumulation, so daily weights detect worsening overload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How do loop diuretics benefit patients with heart failure?","choices":["They relieve symptoms by lowering preload","They slow disease progression","They prolong survival","They reverse ventricular remodeling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less sodium and water retention lowers preload and gives symptomatic benefit. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which non-cardiac illness can precipitate decompensation in heart failure?","choices":["Weight loss","Regular exercise","Pulmonary infection","A low-sodium diet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary infection, emotional stress and acute anginal chest pain are listed precipitants. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which effect on the ventricle do angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors have in heart failure?","choices":["More remodeling and dilation","More sympathetic activation","Thicker ventricular walls","Less hypertrophy and remodeling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They decrease left ventricular hypertrophy, dilation and remodeling, which slows progression. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which defect characterizes systolic dysfunction?","choices":["Impaired relaxation","Decreased contractility","Excess blood leaving the ventricle","A failure of the valves to open"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systolic dysfunction is a failure of contraction, assessed as a reduced ejection fraction. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which diuretic class is the mainstay of heart failure therapy?","choices":["Thiazide diuretics","Loop diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics are the mainstay for relieving congestion in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which three beta blockers reduce mortality in heart failure?","choices":["Metoprolol tartrate, atenolol, nadolol","Propranolol, labetalol, acebutolol","Esmolol, timolol, labetalol","Carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These three have been shown to lower mortality in heart failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How should a beta blocker be started in a patient with heart failure?","choices":["At the full target dose","At a low dose, stopped once symptoms ease","At a full dose after a loading period","At a very low dose, then raised slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are started at very low doses and titrated up slowly while watching for worsening failure. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What should be monitored after a beta blocker is started in heart failure?","choices":["A serum digoxin level","The international normalized ratio","Signs and symptoms of worsening failure","Serum uric acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Worsening heart failure is the main risk after starting, so signs and symptoms are monitored. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Beta blockers decrease the need for which procedure in heart failure?","choices":["Coronary stenting","Valve replacement","Pacemaker placement","Heart transplant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers decrease the need for transplant, along with hospitalizations and mortality. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How do loop diuretics and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors differ in their effect on serum potassium?","choices":["Both lower it","Both raise it","Loops lower it and the inhibitors raise it","Loops raise it and the inhibitors lower it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loops increase potassium excretion, while angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors elevate serum potassium. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which change leads to systolic dysfunction?","choices":["Impaired relaxation from trapped calcium","Faster removal of calcium from the cytosol","Loss of myocardial muscle mass","Longer time for ventricular filling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loss of muscle mass, as after an infarction, and dilated cardiomyopathies lower contractility. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which benefit is expected from an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor in heart failure?","choices":["Better exercise tolerance","Greater force of contraction","A faster heart rate","Higher sympathetic tone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These drugs improve exercise tolerance, with fewer admissions and longer survival. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which hemodynamic effect do angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors have in heart failure?","choices":["Lower both preload and afterload","Raise preload and lower afterload","Lower preload and raise afterload","Raise both preload and afterload"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These drugs decrease preload and afterload and also lower sympathetic activation. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which compensatory response in heart failure increases afterload?","choices":["Sodium and water retention","Vasoconstriction","Increased contractility","Tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vasoconstriction raises the resistance the ventricle pumps against, or afterload. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
